{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-122316",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14097535/assets/2093298b6cacb0b29f874d401ccca40560517ff5887763e9a2b88bf4bc051abb.png)\n\nWe're given that the velocity function $v(x)$ is\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}} \\\\ (1+x)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1 \\\\ \\int (1+x)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int (1+x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}(1+x)^{3} & =t+K \\\\ (1+x)^{3} & =3t+C \\\\ 1+x & =\\sqrt[3]{3t+C} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt[3]{3t+C}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=0$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=1,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\sqrt[3]{3t+1}-1.\n$$",
